<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Batum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus-campaign">Caucasus campaign</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batum</a> was signed on 4 June 1918 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the new republics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> after the collapse of the Transcaucasian Federation. It forced Armenia to cede large territories, leaving it a small landlocked state of about 10,000 square kilometres around Erivan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-sevan">Lake Sevan</a>, crowded with refugees. It was overturned by the Ottoman defeat and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-mudros">Armistice of Mudros</a> in October 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-batum.jpg" alt="Treaty of Batum" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peachy1621 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batum_Treaty.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 5 December 1917 the armistice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a> ended the fighting between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in the Caucasus Campaign, and Russia left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> on 3 March 1918 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a>. From 14 March to April 1918 the Ottoman Empire and a delegation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transcaucasian-sejm">Transcaucasian Sejm</a>, the ruling body of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a>, held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a> peace conference. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha">Enver Pasha</a> offered to give up all Ottoman ambitions in the Caucasus in return for recognition of the Ottoman reacquisition of the east Anatolian provinces.</p><p>On 5 April the head of the Transcaucasian delegation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akaki-chkhenkeli">Akaki Chkhenkeli</a>, accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a> as a basis for negotiation. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> the mood was very different, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> pressed the Commissariat to refuse. Hostilities resumed, and Ottoman troops overran new lands to the east and reached the prewar borders. About 40,000 civilians died during the retreat of Armenian and Georgian volunteers and the Ottoman advance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>A new conference opened at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batum</a> on 11 May. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> widened their demands to include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>, Alexandropol and Echmiadzin, and wanted a railway built to connect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kars">Kars</a> and Julfa with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, with free right of passage through the new Armenian state. The Armenian and Georgian delegates began to stall. From 21 May the Ottoman army advanced again into Russian Armenia, which led to the battles of Sardarapat, Karakilisa and Bash Abaran.</p><p>The treaty, with 14 articles, was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batumi">Batumi</a> on 4 June 1918 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and the First Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, the Azerbaijan Democratic Republic and the Democratic Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. It was the first treaty of the Armenian and Azerbaijani republics. The Ottoman signatories included Halil Menteshe and Wehib Pasha, the Armenian Avetis Aharonian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-khatisian">Alexander Khatisian</a>, the Azerbaijani Mammad Amin Rasulzade and the Georgian Noe Ramishvili.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Ottoman Third Army held positions 7 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a> and 10 km from Echmiadzin when the treaty was signed. The majority of the 1,250,000 prewar inhabitants of the surrendered territories had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, with more than 400,000 in the ceded sector of Yerevan province alone. The treaty left the Armenians with less than one ninth of Eastern Armenia. The German diplomat Bernstorff remarked that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> had left the Armenians only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-sevan">Lake Sevan</a>, where they could swim but had no place to come out and dry off.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Batum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Batum</b> in June 1918 left the new state a small territory around Erivan, crowded with refugees from the Armenian genocide.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-republic-of-armenia">First Republic of Armenia</a></li></ul></section>
